Also, there is a [suggestion](https://internals.rust-lang.org/t/should-we-change-fetch-logic-op-for-atomicbool-to-exploit-knowledge-about-it-contents/20098/9) to use `compare_exchange` here to remove branching at all.
It also always has SeqCst semantics on x86 regardless of success and ordering provided by caller.

```rust
fn fetch_and(a: &AtomicBool, val: bool, order: Ordering)->bool {
    let (Ok(v)|Err(v)) = a.compare_exchange(true, val, SeqCst, SeqCst);
    v
}

fn fetch_or(a: &AtomicBool, val: bool, order: Ordering)->bool {
    let (Ok(v)|Err(v)) = a.compare_exchange(false, val, SeqCst, SeqCst);
    v
}
```

Another thing is `fetch_xor` where I can remove loop for `false` argument but I think that it is better to avoid code duplication.

