I think it is perfectly valid for users to assume that a static's address will not get erased during intermediate stages. Even if it is not valid to assume that a generic const ever constains any meaningful addresses.

```rust
static FOO: usize = 42;
const BAR: &usize = &FOO;
fn foo<const X: &usize>() {
    if X.addr() == FOO.addr() {
        println!("activating special mode");
    }
}

foo::<BAR>();
```

That seems extremely sketchy to me, const generics do not have reference identity.

I don't think this is any worse than
```rust
const BAR: (&usize, &usize) = {
  let val = &0; (val, val)
};
fn foo<const X: (&usize, &usize)>() {
    if X.0.addr() == X.1.addr() {
        println!("activating special mode");
    }
}

foo::<BAR>();
```

