This PR delays some `normalize` until after we instantiate a binder. Because of this we can now replace previously higher ranked projections with inference variables.

As we previously did not replaced these projections with inference variables, we incorrectly relied on structural equality when relating them, guiding type inference.

For an example, see https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/119849#discussion_r1448812002

```rust
pub trait Scalar: 'static {
    type RefType<'a>: ScalarRef<'a>;
}

pub trait ScalarRef<'a>: 'a {}

fn cmp_eq<'a, 'b, A: Scalar, B: Scalar, O: Scalar>(a: A::RefType<'a>, b: B::RefType<'b>) -> O {
    todo!()
}

fn build_expression<A: Scalar, B: Scalar, O: Scalar>(
) -> impl Fn(A::RefType<'_>, B::RefType<'_>) -> O {
    cmp_eq
    //~^ ERROR type annotations needed
}

fn main() {}
```
> This previously compiled because of incompleteness when proving `for<'a, 'b> cmp_eq<?0, ?1, ?2>: Fn(A::RefType<'a>, B::RefType<'b>) -> O`
> 
> This constrained `?0` to `A` even though there could have been another type for which `<?x as Scalar>::RefType<'a> eq <A as Scalar>::RefType<'a>>` holds, this is related to https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/102048 and is fixed because we now normalize `for<'a> <?x as Scalar>::RefType<'a>` after instantiating the binder, causing it to be replaced with a fresh infer var.

I consider this to clearly be a bug fix, making higher ranked projections slightly less broken. It also matches the behavior of the new solver, which handles higher ranked projections correctly due to deferred projection equality.

Crater found a single regression for which I have opened a fix:  koute/polkavm#93

