@cjgillot  You can find my attempt to redesign here: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/commit/9b885f1dc236febb3fcfe2b72219ec39cc55e208 . I'm actually quite happy with it.

Just like https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/120193 , every `SwitchInt` has `cold_targets` vector, which is used in the backend.

Where it differs is how the vector is filled. https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/120193 tried to fill the vector when lowering AST, but then the information needs to be preserved during opt passes, which turns out to be difficult. It also makes maintenance of the passes more difficult. For instance, consider the `jump threading` pass:
```rust
//! This optimization seeks to replace join-then-switch control flow patterns by straight jumps
//!    X = 0                                      X = 0
//! ------------\      /--------              ------------
//!    X = 1     X----X SwitchInt(X)     =>       X = 1
//! ------------/      \--------              ------------
```
When replacing the `SwitchInt` with jumps, I would need to distribute the `cold_targets` to upstream `SwitchInt`s... possibly more than one

In order to avoid this, I decided to add a `cold_path` intrinsic and implement `likely`/`unlikely` in terms of `cold_path`:
```rust
#[cold]
fn cold_path() {}

#[inline(always)]
fn likely(x: bool) -> bool {
	if x {
		true
	} else {
		cold_path();
		false
	}
}
```
Opt passes can remain unchanged. They only need to preserve the call to `cold_path`. After opt passes are finished, I run a simple pass that looks for the `cold_path()` intrinsics and fills the `cold_target` vector of each `SwitchInt`. The pass has linear complexity.

At the moment, the `cold_path` is just an empty cold function. The assumption is that the mir inline pass will not inline it, so we can detect it. If the behavior of the inline pass changed, we would need to make it a `NonDivergentIntrinsic`

It generates nice code. One of the reasons is the `jump threading` pass, which will merge the condition inside `likely`/`unlikely` with the condition using the intrinsics.

Also, in https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/120193 I implemented the following rewrites in the `then_else_break()` function:
```rust
likely(lhs && rhs) => likely(lhs) && likely(rhs)
unlikely(lhs && rhs) => lhs && unlikely(rhs)
likely(lhs || rhs) => lhs || likely(rhs)
unlikely(lhs || rhs) => unlikely(lhs) && unlikely(rhs)
```
With this redesign, I didn't have to implement them. They are a side effect of the algorithm.

Plus if we use this redesign, the implementation of https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/120193 would actually become really simple. I could throw away the change to the match algorithm, which is already quite complex, and simply put the `cold_path` intrinsics as a first thing in the cold arms. So the following:
```rust
match x {
    #[cold] 10 => { ... }
    _ => { ... }
}
```
would be rewritten as:
```rust
match x {
    10 => { cold_path(); ... }
    _ => { ... }
}
```
One disadvantage is that this new design doesn't work for `unpredictable`. That intrinsic would need to be implemented separately.


But enough of me selling the redesign. Please let me know what you think :-)

> Note that LLVM recommends against emitting branch weights ourselves in [llvm/llvm-project@e3a4701](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/commit/e3a470162738871bba982416748ae5f5e3572947). We should emit `expect` intrinsics as before.

