> The inline pass in Rust will never inline a function with this attribute. As I understand, this is because at the time the inline pass runs, it doesn't know if the backend will implement the intrinsic. But if they are not inlined and replaced with `cold_path`, they will not work

There is a way to overcome this limitation, but it is not for this PR. We'd have to do some analysis in MIR or HIR to recognize `if likely` and `if unlikely`, then store which value is expected in the `SwitchInt` terminator. But that sounds rather fragile to me, and doesn't work at all with `match` so I wonder if the right API here is actually `cold_path`.

@bors r+ rollup=never

@rustbot label: +perf-regression-triaged

Some change is expected because these intrinsics used to do nothing. Presumably they are used in the ecosystem, we have a few uses in the compiler too, so compiler perf here is a balance of maybe the uses in the compiler help, maybe they don't, and it's probably more work for LLVM to handle the improved IR.

Overall, strong positive on primary benchmarks and also on check builds so I do not think there is any required follow-up work. Might be fun to tune our uses of these intrinsics, I would think they're unnecessary in the compiler because we use PGO but who knows.

